WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.704
In this video, we'll learn coordinates
on a flat map the easy way,

00:00:04.704 --> 00:00:09.975
and see how meteorology uses them to
measure the winds round a hurricane's eye.

00:00:10.775 --> 00:00:14.675
What are coordinates, and why
do we need more than one kind?

00:00:15.025 --> 00:00:18.448
This is the first of seven
parts on coordinates,

00:00:18.448 --> 00:00:21.300
and in this part, we stay on a flat map.

00:00:21.650 --> 00:00:25.900
Let's see what coordinates are, and why
they're built the way they are.

00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:30.475
Then let's see what they tell us
about the winds round a hurricane.

00:00:31.275 --> 00:00:33.575
Let's start with a flat map.

00:00:33.925 --> 00:00:37.523
To say where something
is, we need a starting point,

00:00:37.523 --> 00:00:39.875
and some directions to count from.

00:00:40.225 --> 00:00:43.225
The starting point is called the origin.

00:00:43.575 --> 00:00:46.050
Let's put it at a weather station.

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:50.975
Now draw one line through it pointing
east, and one pointing north.

00:00:51.325 --> 00:00:53.900
These lines are called the axes.

00:00:54.250 --> 00:00:57.741
A weather balloon drifts
three kilometres east and

00:00:57.741 --> 00:01:00.325
four kilometres north of the station.

00:01:00.675 --> 00:01:05.100
So we can write its position
as the pair of numbers three and four.

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:09.491
These two numbers are
called its coordinates,

00:01:09.491 --> 00:01:12.575
and together they pin
down one place on the map.

00:01:12.925 --> 00:01:16.794
Measuring along straight axes
at right angles, like this,

00:01:16.794 --> 00:01:21.750
is called Cartesian coordinates,
after the French thinker René Descartes.

00:01:22.550 --> 00:01:28.200
Each axis comes with a unit vector: an
arrow of length one that points along it.

00:01:28.550 --> 00:01:31.539
We write x hat
for the arrow pointing east,

00:01:31.539 --> 00:01:34.250
and y hat for the arrow pointing north.

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:37.884
The balloon's position
is three times x hat,

00:01:37.884 --> 00:01:43.407
plus four times y hat. Three steps
of x hat take us three kilometres east,

00:01:43.407 --> 00:01:47.438
and four steps of y hat take
us four kilometres north,

00:01:47.438 --> 00:01:50.050
which lands exactly on the balloon.

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:54.525
We met arrows like these
in the vectors movie of this series.

00:01:54.875 --> 00:01:59.925
Here's their key property: x hat points
the same way everywhere on the map.

00:02:00.275 --> 00:02:04.725
Move the balloon anywhere you
like, and the two arrows don't change.

00:02:05.075 --> 00:02:09.200
That's what makes Cartesian
coordinates so easy to work with.

00:02:09.550 --> 00:02:13.150
But many things in the weather
aren't laid out in squares.

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:18.650
A hurricane spins round its eye, and
a weather radar looks out in rings.

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:22.675
For those, there's a better
way to say where something is.

00:02:23.475 --> 00:02:27.748
Instead of going east and north, we
can give two other numbers:

00:02:27.748 --> 00:02:31.275
how far away the balloon
is, and in which direction.

00:02:31.625 --> 00:02:34.550
The distance from the origin is called r.

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:35.925
By Pythagoras,

00:02:35.925 --> 00:02:41.706
r is the square root of three squared
plus four squared. Three squared is nine,

00:02:41.706 --> 00:02:43.755
and four squared is sixteen,

00:02:43.755 --> 00:02:48.000
so that's the square root
of twenty five: five kilometres.

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:52.650
The direction is an angle, called theta.

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:56.362
Mathematicians measure
it from the east axis,

00:02:56.362 --> 00:02:59.425
turning anticlockwise as seen from above.

00:02:59.775 --> 00:03:01.170
In a right triangle,

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:03.612
the tangent of an angle is the side

00:03:03.612 --> 00:03:06.611
opposite it divided by
the side next to it:

00:03:06.611 --> 00:03:09.331
here the north side over the east side,

00:03:09.331 --> 00:03:10.448
four over three,

00:03:10.448 --> 00:03:13.796
about one point three three.
The angle with that

00:03:13.796 --> 00:03:17.074
tangent is about fifty
three point one degrees,

00:03:17.074 --> 00:03:19.725
so theta is about fifty three degrees.

00:03:21.225 --> 00:03:24.891
So the same balloon
is at r equals five kilometres,

00:03:24.891 --> 00:03:27.550
and theta equals fifty three degrees.

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:31.200
These two numbers are
called polar coordinates.

00:03:31.550 --> 00:03:34.567
A weather radar measures
exactly this pair:

00:03:34.567 --> 00:03:38.425
how far away the raindrops
are, and in which direction.

00:03:39.225 --> 00:03:41.525
Can we go back the other way?

00:03:41.875 --> 00:03:43.225
Yes.

00:03:43.575 --> 00:03:47.050
Think of the right triangle
with the balloon at its tip.

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:51.875
Its long side is r, and its angle
at the station is theta.

00:03:52.225 --> 00:03:55.585
The side going east
is r times cosine theta,

00:03:55.585 --> 00:03:59.175
and the side going north
is r times sine theta.

00:03:59.525 --> 00:04:01.100
Let's check.

00:04:01.450 --> 00:04:05.570
Cosine theta is the east side
divided by the long side,

00:04:05.570 --> 00:04:09.091
and sine theta is the north
side divided by the

00:04:09.091 --> 00:04:12.612
long side. For our balloon
that's three fifths,

00:04:12.612 --> 00:04:15.009
zero point six, and four fifths,

00:04:15.009 --> 00:04:21.076
zero point eight. So five times the cosine
of theta is five times zero point six,

00:04:21.076 --> 00:04:22.200
which is three.

00:04:22.550 --> 00:04:25.425
And five times zero point eight is four.

00:04:26.925 --> 00:04:31.775
So x equals r cosine
theta, and y equals r sine theta.

00:04:32.125 --> 00:04:36.550
These two lines are all we need
to switch between the two systems.

00:04:37.350 --> 00:04:39.825
One warning before we go on.

00:04:40.175 --> 00:04:44.825
Meteorologists and navigators
measure directions differently.

00:04:45.175 --> 00:04:50.300
A compass bearing starts from north, and
turns clockwise as seen from above.

00:04:50.650 --> 00:04:54.306
So the balloon's bearing
is ninety minus fifty three,

00:04:54.306 --> 00:04:56.375
which is thirty seven degrees.

00:04:57.875 --> 00:05:00.322
Weather reports add one more twist:

00:05:00.322 --> 00:05:03.818
a wind's direction says
where the wind blows from,

00:05:03.818 --> 00:05:06.475
as we saw in the weather basics movie.

00:05:06.825 --> 00:05:09.725
So a west wind blows towards the east.

00:05:10.075 --> 00:05:12.231
Same arrows, different rulers,

00:05:12.231 --> 00:05:16.400
so it's always worth checking
which angle a data set uses.

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:20.569
Before polar coordinates
can do their real work,

00:05:20.569 --> 00:05:24.850
we need one more building block:
measuring angles in radians.

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:29.150
Take a circle of radius r, and walk
along its edge.

00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:34.700
The angle in radians is the distance
you walked, divided by the radius.

00:05:35.050 --> 00:05:41.000
So the distance along the edge, called the
arc length, is the radius times the angle.

00:05:41.350 --> 00:05:46.925
A full circle is two pi radians, because
its edge is two pi times the radius.

00:05:47.275 --> 00:05:52.302
Two pi is about six point two eight, so
one radian is three hundred and

00:05:52.302 --> 00:05:57.400
sixty degrees divided by six point two
eight: about fifty seven degrees.

00:05:57.750 --> 00:06:00.025
Let's try easy numbers.

00:06:00.375 --> 00:06:03.576
On a ring five kilometres
from the station,

00:06:03.576 --> 00:06:07.670
turn by zero point two radians:
that's about eleven and

00:06:07.670 --> 00:06:13.550
a half degrees. It walks you five times
zero point two, which is one kilometre.

00:06:15.050 --> 00:06:20.150
On a ring fifty kilometres out, the same
turn walks you ten kilometres.

00:06:21.650 --> 00:06:24.975
The same angle gives
ten times the distance.

00:06:25.325 --> 00:06:30.350
Keep this rule in mind, because it
explains a lot about maps of the Earth.

00:06:31.150 --> 00:06:34.585
Polar coordinates come
with their own unit vectors,

00:06:34.585 --> 00:06:36.875
so let's build them one at a time.

00:06:37.225 --> 00:06:39.525
The first is called r hat.

00:06:39.875 --> 00:06:43.750
It's an arrow of length one that
points away from the station,

00:06:43.750 --> 00:06:45.000
towards the balloon.

00:06:45.350 --> 00:06:47.175
Here's how to get it.

00:06:47.525 --> 00:06:51.160
The arrow from the station
to the balloon is three,

00:06:51.160 --> 00:06:54.225
four, and its length
is r, five kilometres.

00:06:54.575 --> 00:07:00.025
Dividing an arrow by its own length keeps
its direction, and makes its length one.

00:07:00.375 --> 00:07:05.048
So r hat is three fifths, four
fifths, which is zero point six,

00:07:05.048 --> 00:07:08.533
zero point eight. We
draw r hat at the balloon,

00:07:08.533 --> 00:07:11.500
because it tells us the direction there.

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:18.145
Its length is the square root
of zero point six squared plus zero point

00:07:18.145 --> 00:07:23.072
eight squared. That's zero point three six
plus zero point six four,

00:07:23.072 --> 00:07:26.550
which is one, and the square
root of one is one.

00:07:26.900 --> 00:07:29.150
The same works for any point.

00:07:29.500 --> 00:07:34.750
The arrow to it is r cosine
theta, r sine theta, as we just saw.

00:07:35.100 --> 00:07:40.775
Dividing by r, the r cancels,
so r hat is cosine theta, sine theta.

00:07:42.275 --> 00:07:44.450
Why is it called r hat?

00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:47.084
Hold theta fixed and let r grow,

00:07:47.084 --> 00:07:51.225
and the balloon slides straight
outward, along this arrow.

00:07:51.575 --> 00:07:54.375
So r hat points the way r grows.

00:07:55.175 --> 00:07:59.083
The next few minutes build
theta hat one step at a time,

00:07:59.083 --> 00:08:01.875
for anyone who enjoys seeing every move.

00:08:02.225 --> 00:08:05.926
You don't need these steps
for the intuition of weather,

00:08:05.926 --> 00:08:07.512
climate science or maps,

00:08:07.512 --> 00:08:11.675
so if it feels like too much, skip
ahead to the time on screen.

00:08:12.025 --> 00:08:14.775
Now the second arrow, theta hat.

00:08:15.125 --> 00:08:18.225
Its job is to point the way theta grows.

00:08:18.575 --> 00:08:22.300
So let's do the same experiment,
with the roles swapped.

00:08:22.650 --> 00:08:27.300
Hold r fixed at five kilometres,
and let theta grow a little.

00:08:27.650 --> 00:08:33.075
The balloon slides along the circle of
radius five kilometres round the station.

00:08:33.425 --> 00:08:35.775
Which way is that, exactly?

00:08:36.125 --> 00:08:40.425
While it slides, its distance
from the station doesn't change.

00:08:40.775 --> 00:08:43.344
If its move had any part along r hat,

00:08:43.344 --> 00:08:48.689
the balloon would get further from the
station, or closer to it. For example,

00:08:48.689 --> 00:08:52.160
with a part of a tenth
of a kilometre along r hat,

00:08:52.160 --> 00:08:57.575
it would end up five point one or four
point nine kilometres from the station,

00:08:57.575 --> 00:09:03.098
not five. So a small slide along
the circle has no part along r hat:

00:09:03.098 --> 00:09:05.450
it's at right angles to r hat.

00:09:05.800 --> 00:09:11.075
That's why the edge of a circle always
runs at right angles to its radius.

00:09:11.425 --> 00:09:15.332
But there are two directions
at right angles to r hat:

00:09:15.332 --> 00:09:19.600
one turned anticlockwise
from it, and one turned clockwise.

00:09:19.950 --> 00:09:23.512
Theta grows anticlockwise,
as seen from above,

00:09:23.512 --> 00:09:26.300
so the balloon slides anticlockwise.

00:09:26.650 --> 00:09:31.850
So theta hat is r hat, turned by ninety
degrees anticlockwise.

00:09:33.350 --> 00:09:37.479
To turn that picture into numbers,
we need one small tool:

00:09:37.479 --> 00:09:40.825
a rule for turning any
arrow by ninety degrees.

00:09:41.625 --> 00:09:44.500
Let's start with the two easiest arrows.

00:09:44.850 --> 00:09:47.055
Turn x hat, which points east,

00:09:47.055 --> 00:09:50.950
by ninety degrees anticlockwise,
and it points north.

00:09:51.300 --> 00:09:53.625
So it becomes zero, one.

00:09:55.125 --> 00:09:58.875
Turn y hat, which points
north, and it points west.

00:09:59.225 --> 00:10:02.075
So it becomes minus one, zero.

00:10:03.575 --> 00:10:08.175
Now take any arrow, with an east
part p and a north part q.

00:10:08.525 --> 00:10:13.575
It's p steps along x hat,
followed by q steps along y hat.

00:10:13.925 --> 00:10:17.178
If we turn the whole
drawing by ninety degrees,

00:10:17.178 --> 00:10:21.400
both pieces turn together,
and they stay joined, tip to tail.

00:10:21.750 --> 00:10:28.275
So the p steps east become p steps north,
and the q steps north become q steps west.

00:10:28.625 --> 00:10:32.450
Steps to the west count
as minus in the east part.

00:10:32.800 --> 00:10:38.795
p times zero, one is zero, p, and q times
minus one, zero is minus q,

00:10:38.795 --> 00:10:42.444
zero. Adding the east parts gives minus q,

00:10:42.444 --> 00:10:48.525
and adding the north parts gives p.
So the turned arrow is minus q, p.

00:10:50.025 --> 00:10:55.025
In words: swap the two numbers,
and put a minus sign on the first one.

00:10:55.375 --> 00:10:58.825
Let's try it on the balloon's
arrow, three, four.

00:10:59.175 --> 00:11:03.450
Swap them, and put a minus on the first:
minus four, three.

00:11:04.950 --> 00:11:07.300
Is that really a quarter turn?

00:11:07.650 --> 00:11:11.525
Minus four squared is sixteen
and three squared is nine,

00:11:11.525 --> 00:11:14.846
so its length is the square
root of twenty five:

00:11:14.846 --> 00:11:17.475
five, the same as the balloon's arrow.

00:11:17.825 --> 00:11:22.298
And its dot product with three, four
is three times minus four,

00:11:22.298 --> 00:11:24.925
plus four times three, which is zero.

00:11:26.425 --> 00:11:32.050
A zero dot product means a right
angle, as we saw in the dot product movie.

00:11:32.850 --> 00:11:35.225
Now let's turn r hat itself.

00:11:35.575 --> 00:11:40.400
Its east part is cosine
theta, and its north part is sine theta.

00:11:40.750 --> 00:11:44.875
Using the rule, we swap them
and put a minus on the first.

00:11:45.225 --> 00:11:49.225
So theta hat is minus sine
theta, cosine theta.

00:11:50.725 --> 00:11:53.250
Here's the same thing as a picture.

00:11:53.600 --> 00:11:57.325
Turn r hat's little
triangle by ninety degrees.

00:11:57.675 --> 00:12:01.314
Its east side, cosine
theta, now points north,

00:12:01.314 --> 00:12:04.400
so cosine theta becomes the north part.

00:12:04.750 --> 00:12:08.242
Its north side, sine
theta, now points west,

00:12:08.242 --> 00:12:11.575
so minus sine theta becomes the east part.

00:12:11.925 --> 00:12:14.350
Let's check that this makes sense.

00:12:14.700 --> 00:12:16.417
At theta equals zero,

00:12:16.417 --> 00:12:21.487
the point is due east of the station.
Minus sine zero is zero,

00:12:21.487 --> 00:12:25.575
and cosine zero is one, so
theta hat is zero, one.

00:12:25.925 --> 00:12:30.675
It points north, which is the way
the point slides when theta grows.

00:12:32.175 --> 00:12:38.267
At ninety degrees, the point is due
north. Minus sine ninety is minus one,

00:12:38.267 --> 00:12:43.125
and cosine ninety is zero, so
theta hat is minus one, zero.

00:12:43.475 --> 00:12:45.275
It points west.

00:12:46.775 --> 00:12:51.900
At the balloon, theta hat is minus
zero point eight, zero point six.

00:12:53.400 --> 00:12:54.666
Its length is one,

00:12:54.666 --> 00:12:57.761
and its dot product
with r hat is minus zero

00:12:57.761 --> 00:13:00.857
point four eight
plus zero point four eight,

00:13:00.857 --> 00:13:03.600
which is zero, so it's at right angles.

00:13:03.950 --> 00:13:08.200
And one last check, without
using the theta hat formula.

00:13:08.550 --> 00:13:11.995
The balloon is five
kilometres from the station:

00:13:11.995 --> 00:13:14.650
three kilometres east and four north.

00:13:15.000 --> 00:13:17.275
Turn it by a tenth of a degree.

00:13:17.625 --> 00:13:21.812
In radians, that's a tenth
times pi over one hundred and

00:13:21.812 --> 00:13:25.925
eighty, about
zero point zero zero one seven four five.

00:13:27.425 --> 00:13:31.291
By the arc rule, s equals r times
the change in theta,

00:13:31.291 --> 00:13:34.513
so it slides five thousand
metres times that:

00:13:34.513 --> 00:13:37.950
about eight point seven
metres along the circle.

00:13:39.450 --> 00:13:45.650
Its position comes from x equals r cosine
theta, and y equals r sine theta.

00:13:46.000 --> 00:13:50.841
It moves from three thousand metres
east and four thousand metres north,

00:13:50.841 --> 00:13:53.396
to about two thousand nine hundred and

00:13:53.396 --> 00:13:56.825
ninety three, and four
thousand and five point two.

00:13:57.175 --> 00:13:59.481
That's about seven metres west,

00:13:59.481 --> 00:14:04.540
and five point two metres north. Check:
seven squared is forty nine,

00:14:04.540 --> 00:14:09.747
and five point two squared is twenty
seven point zero four. The square

00:14:09.747 --> 00:14:14.434
root of seventy six point zero four
is about eight point seven:

00:14:14.434 --> 00:14:15.550
the same slide.

00:14:17.050 --> 00:14:22.264
Dividing each by eight point seven
gives about minus zero point eight and

00:14:22.264 --> 00:14:24.550
zero point six: theta hat again.

00:14:26.050 --> 00:14:30.976
If you skipped, here's what to keep:
r hat points away from the centre,

00:14:30.976 --> 00:14:33.820
theta hat points round it, anticlockwise,

00:14:33.820 --> 00:14:37.150
and theta hat is r hat
turned by ninety degrees.

00:14:37.950 --> 00:14:42.475
Now let's move the balloon round
the circle, and watch both arrows.

00:14:42.825 --> 00:14:48.125
Due east of the station, r hat points
east, and theta hat points north.

00:14:48.475 --> 00:14:52.775
Due north, r hat points
north, and theta hat points west.

00:14:53.125 --> 00:14:57.475
Due west, r hat points
west, and theta hat points south.

00:14:57.825 --> 00:15:01.936
So r hat and theta hat turn
as the balloon goes round,

00:15:01.936 --> 00:15:04.600
while x hat and y hat never change.

00:15:04.950 --> 00:15:08.324
That's the big difference
between the two systems:

00:15:08.324 --> 00:15:11.225
polar unit vectors
depend on where you are.

00:15:11.575 --> 00:15:17.000
Keep this in mind, because these turning
arrows will matter a lot in Part 2.

00:15:17.800 --> 00:15:21.464
The next two scenes split a wind
into its polar parts,

00:15:21.464 --> 00:15:24.925
number by number, for learners
who want every step.

00:15:25.275 --> 00:15:29.639
If you'd rather keep the big picture,
skip ahead to the time on screen;

00:15:29.639 --> 00:15:33.450
you won't miss anything you need
for weather, climate or maps.

00:15:33.800 --> 00:15:37.575
Now let's describe a wind
in polar coordinates.

00:15:37.925 --> 00:15:43.250
Suppose that at the balloon, the wind
blows due east at ten metres per second.

00:15:43.600 --> 00:15:46.075
We'll call the wind arrow bold u.

00:15:46.425 --> 00:15:50.725
Its eastward part
is called u, and its northward part v.

00:15:51.075 --> 00:15:53.825
Here, u is ten and v is zero.

00:15:55.325 --> 00:16:00.600
How much of this wind blows away from
the station, and how much blows round it?

00:16:00.950 --> 00:16:05.500
The part along r hat is called
the radial wind, u sub r.

00:16:05.850 --> 00:16:11.050
The part along theta hat is called
the tangential wind, u sub theta.

00:16:11.400 --> 00:16:16.789
To find each one, we take the dot product
of the wind with that unit vector,

00:16:16.789 --> 00:16:18.775
as in the dot product movie.

00:16:19.125 --> 00:16:23.100
It gives the length of the wind's
shadow along that arrow.

00:16:23.450 --> 00:16:25.350
Here are the inputs.

00:16:25.700 --> 00:16:28.125
The wind is ten, zero.

00:16:28.475 --> 00:16:31.725
The angle theta is fifty three degrees.

00:16:32.075 --> 00:16:35.332
R hat is zero point six, zero point eight,

00:16:35.332 --> 00:16:39.675
and theta hat is minus
zero point eight, zero point six.

00:16:40.025 --> 00:16:42.450
First, the radial wind.

00:16:42.800 --> 00:16:45.556
Multiply the matching parts, and add:

00:16:45.556 --> 00:16:49.950
ten times zero point six, plus
zero times zero point eight.

00:16:50.300 --> 00:16:55.125
That's six plus zero, so u sub r is six
metres per second.

00:16:56.625 --> 00:17:01.372
Next, the tangential wind:
ten times minus zero point eight,

00:17:01.372 --> 00:17:03.825
plus zero times zero point six.

00:17:04.175 --> 00:17:09.975
That's minus eight plus zero, so u sub
theta is minus eight metres per second.

00:17:11.475 --> 00:17:16.875
So six metres per second blow away
from the station, and eight blow round it.

00:17:17.225 --> 00:17:21.617
The minus sign means the wind
goes round against theta hat:

00:17:21.617 --> 00:17:23.850
clockwise, as seen from above.

00:17:24.650 --> 00:17:28.500
Let's check that these two parts
really make up the same wind.

00:17:28.850 --> 00:17:33.900
We rebuild it: six times r hat, plus
minus eight times theta hat.

00:17:34.250 --> 00:17:40.150
Six times zero point six, zero point eight
is three point six, four point eight.

00:17:41.650 --> 00:17:46.038
Minus eight times minus zero point eight
is plus six point four,

00:17:46.038 --> 00:17:48.506
because two minus signs make a plus,

00:17:48.506 --> 00:17:52.825
and minus eight times zero point six
is minus four point eight.

00:17:54.325 --> 00:17:59.775
Adding the east parts gives three point
six plus six point four, which is ten.

00:18:00.125 --> 00:18:04.950
The north parts give four point eight
minus four point eight, which is zero.

00:18:06.450 --> 00:18:11.325
We're back to ten, zero: the same
wind, measured with new arrows.

00:18:11.675 --> 00:18:15.110
Its length agrees too:
six squared is thirty six and

00:18:15.110 --> 00:18:16.960
eight squared is sixty four,

00:18:16.960 --> 00:18:22.575
which makes a hundred, and its square root
is ten: the same as the wind's own length.

00:18:24.075 --> 00:18:28.275
The same dot products work
for any wind and any angle.

00:18:28.625 --> 00:18:32.994
With the wind u, v, and r hat
equal to cosine theta,

00:18:32.994 --> 00:18:37.700
sine theta, u sub r is u cosine
theta plus v sine theta.

00:18:39.200 --> 00:18:43.847
And with theta hat equal
to minus sine theta, cosine theta,

00:18:43.847 --> 00:18:48.100
u sub theta is minus u sine
theta plus v cosine theta.

00:18:49.600 --> 00:18:53.200
We'll use these two lines
on a real hurricane soon.

00:18:53.550 --> 00:18:56.126
If you skipped, here's what to keep:

00:18:56.126 --> 00:18:59.775
any wind splits into
an in or out part along r hat,

00:18:59.775 --> 00:19:04.425
and a round part along
theta hat, and each part is a dot product.

00:19:05.225 --> 00:19:09.763
The next few scenes turn the axes
and build the rotation matrix,

00:19:09.763 --> 00:19:13.450
one step at a time, for anyone
who wants every step.

00:19:13.800 --> 00:19:16.859
They aren't needed
for the intuition of weather,

00:19:16.859 --> 00:19:21.575
climate science or maps, so feel free
to skip ahead to the time on screen.

00:19:21.925 --> 00:19:25.725
Now let's look at a second
way to change coordinates.

00:19:26.075 --> 00:19:31.200
This time, we keep the origin where
it is, and we just turn the axes.

00:19:31.550 --> 00:19:33.475
Why would we want that?

00:19:33.825 --> 00:19:36.586
Along a coastline, a runway or a valley,

00:19:36.586 --> 00:19:41.900
it's often more useful to measure along
it and across it than east and north.

00:19:42.250 --> 00:19:44.825
The arrow itself stays the same.

00:19:45.175 --> 00:19:48.050
Only the rulers we measure it with turn.

00:19:48.400 --> 00:19:54.650
Say the new x axis is turned by an angle
alpha, anticlockwise as seen from above.

00:19:55.000 --> 00:19:57.900
We call its unit vector x hat prime.

00:19:58.250 --> 00:20:02.500
The little dash is read
prime, and it marks the new axes.

00:20:02.850 --> 00:20:06.658
Like r hat, it's an arrow
of length one at an angle,

00:20:06.658 --> 00:20:09.075
so it's cosine alpha, sine alpha.

00:20:10.575 --> 00:20:13.650
Let's use alpha equals thirty degrees.

00:20:14.000 --> 00:20:18.830
Cosine of thirty degrees
is the square root of three over two:

00:20:18.830 --> 00:20:21.557
one point seven three two over two,

00:20:21.557 --> 00:20:27.400
about zero point eight six six. Sine
of thirty degrees is exactly one half.

00:20:27.750 --> 00:20:32.500
So x hat prime is
zero point eight six six, zero point five.

00:20:34.000 --> 00:20:36.275
What about the new y axis?

00:20:36.625 --> 00:20:39.851
It must be at right
angles to the new x axis,

00:20:39.851 --> 00:20:44.225
and turned anticlockwise
from it, just as north is from east.

00:20:44.575 --> 00:20:48.328
So y hat prime is x hat prime
turned by ninety degrees,

00:20:48.328 --> 00:20:50.920
and we already have the rule for that:

00:20:50.920 --> 00:20:54.400
swap the two numbers,
and put a minus on the first.

00:20:54.750 --> 00:20:58.400
That gives minus sine alpha, cosine alpha.

00:20:59.900 --> 00:21:06.300
With thirty degrees, y hat prime is minus
zero point five, zero point eight six six.

00:21:07.800 --> 00:21:09.450
Let's check it.

00:21:09.800 --> 00:21:15.850
Its length is one, because zero point two
five plus zero point seven five is one.

00:21:16.200 --> 00:21:19.453
And its dot product
with x hat prime is zero

00:21:19.453 --> 00:21:23.001
point eight six six times
minus zero point five,

00:21:23.001 --> 00:21:27.806
plus zero point five times
zero point eight six six. That's minus

00:21:27.806 --> 00:21:32.315
zero point four three three
plus zero point four three three,

00:21:32.315 --> 00:21:33.350
which is zero.

00:21:34.850 --> 00:21:39.175
So the new axes are at right
angles, just like the old ones.

00:21:39.975 --> 00:21:45.000
Now we can measure the balloon's
arrow, three, four, with the new rulers.

00:21:45.350 --> 00:21:49.300
Its new coordinates are
called x prime and y prime.

00:21:49.650 --> 00:21:55.550
X prime answers one question: how much
of the arrow lies along the new x axis?

00:21:55.900 --> 00:21:59.568
That's the length of the arrow's
shadow on that axis,

00:21:59.568 --> 00:22:02.475
which is its dot product with x hat prime.

00:22:02.825 --> 00:22:05.834
Multiply the matching parts, and add:

00:22:05.834 --> 00:22:09.575
x times cosine
alpha, plus y times sine alpha.

00:22:11.075 --> 00:22:16.163
In the same way, y prime
is the dot product with y hat prime:

00:22:16.163 --> 00:22:19.500
minus x sine alpha, plus y cosine alpha.

00:22:21.000 --> 00:22:23.863
Now the numbers, with x equals three,

00:22:23.863 --> 00:22:27.500
y equals four, and alpha
equals thirty degrees.

00:22:27.850 --> 00:22:33.950
X prime is three times zero point eight
six six, plus four times zero point five.

00:22:34.300 --> 00:22:38.300
That's two point six
plus two, about four point six.

00:22:39.800 --> 00:22:43.188
Y prime is minus three
times zero point five,

00:22:43.188 --> 00:22:46.275
plus four times zero point eight six six.

00:22:46.625 --> 00:22:52.425
That's minus one point five plus three
point four six, about one point nine six.

00:22:53.925 --> 00:23:00.025
So in the turned axes, the balloon is at
about four point six, one point nine six.

00:23:00.375 --> 00:23:02.300
Is it the same arrow?

00:23:02.650 --> 00:23:05.591
Its length is the square
root of four point

00:23:05.591 --> 00:23:08.600
six squared
plus one point nine six squared.

00:23:08.950 --> 00:23:12.952
Four point six squared is about
twenty one point one six,

00:23:12.952 --> 00:23:16.111
and one point nine six
squared is about three

00:23:16.111 --> 00:23:19.340
point eight four. Together
that's twenty five,

00:23:19.340 --> 00:23:22.850
and its square root is five
kilometres, as before.

00:23:24.350 --> 00:23:26.300
The arrow didn't move.

00:23:26.650 --> 00:23:29.336
Only the numbers that describe it changed,

00:23:29.336 --> 00:23:32.150
because we measure it
with different rulers.

00:23:32.950 --> 00:23:36.800
These two lines have the same
shape, so let's line them up.

00:23:37.150 --> 00:23:39.202
Write x prime above y prime,

00:23:39.202 --> 00:23:43.600
with the x terms in one column
and the y terms in the other.

00:23:43.950 --> 00:23:47.700
Now look at just the four
numbers in front of x and y.

00:23:48.050 --> 00:23:51.340
The top row is cosine
alpha and sine alpha,

00:23:51.340 --> 00:23:55.625
and the bottom row is minus sine
alpha and cosine alpha.

00:23:55.975 --> 00:23:59.794
That square of four numbers
is called a rotation matrix,

00:23:59.794 --> 00:24:03.000
because it turns old
coordinates into new ones.

00:24:03.350 --> 00:24:06.125
To use it, go along one row.

00:24:06.475 --> 00:24:12.025
Multiply its first number by x, and
its second number by y, then add.

00:24:12.375 --> 00:24:16.975
The top row gives x prime, and
the bottom row gives y prime.

00:24:17.325 --> 00:24:22.642
With our numbers, the top row gives
zero point eight six six times three,

00:24:22.642 --> 00:24:24.973
plus zero point five times four:

00:24:24.973 --> 00:24:30.873
four point six again. And the bottom row
gives minus zero point five times three,

00:24:30.873 --> 00:24:36.700
plus zero point eight six six times four:
one point nine six, our y prime again.

00:24:38.200 --> 00:24:41.250
Each of the four numbers
has a meaning, too.

00:24:41.600 --> 00:24:45.655
The dot product of two arrows
of length one is the cosine

00:24:45.655 --> 00:24:49.568
of the angle between them.
As in the dot product movie:

00:24:49.568 --> 00:24:54.975
length times length times the cosine
of the angle, and both lengths are one.

00:24:55.325 --> 00:25:01.675
So each number is the cosine of the angle
between one new axis and one old axis.

00:25:02.025 --> 00:25:04.925
That's why they're
called direction cosines.

00:25:05.275 --> 00:25:10.290
For example, the angle between x hat
prime and x hat is alpha,

00:25:10.290 --> 00:25:13.282
so their dot product is cosine alpha:

00:25:13.282 --> 00:25:17.650
the top left number. The angle
between x hat prime and

00:25:17.650 --> 00:25:20.562
y hat is ninety degrees minus alpha,

00:25:20.562 --> 00:25:25.657
and its cosine is sine alpha.
The angle between y hat prime and

00:25:25.657 --> 00:25:31.400
x hat is ninety degrees plus alpha, and
its cosine is minus sine alpha.

00:25:32.200 --> 00:25:35.457
In the vectors movie, we
met index notation,

00:25:35.457 --> 00:25:37.900
a short way to write all of this.

00:25:38.250 --> 00:25:40.950
Let's rewrite it one step at a time.

00:25:41.300 --> 00:25:43.175
First, the names.

00:25:43.525 --> 00:25:48.575
The old coordinates x and y become x sub
one and x sub two.

00:25:48.925 --> 00:25:54.264
The old unit vectors x hat and y hat
become e sub one and e sub two,

00:25:54.264 --> 00:25:58.975
and the new ones become e prime
sub one and e prime sub two.

00:25:59.325 --> 00:26:03.100
Next, each direction
cosine gets two labels.

00:26:03.450 --> 00:26:09.675
C sub i j is the dot product of old
arrow number i with new arrow number j.

00:26:10.025 --> 00:26:12.150
Let's work out all four.

00:26:12.500 --> 00:26:17.453
C one one is x hat dot x hat
prime: one times cosine alpha,

00:26:17.453 --> 00:26:21.650
plus zero times sine
alpha, which is cosine alpha.

00:26:23.150 --> 00:26:28.500
C one two is x hat dot y hat
prime, which is minus sine alpha.

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:34.875
C two one is y hat dot x hat
prime, which is sine alpha.

00:26:36.375 --> 00:26:41.675
And C two two is y hat dot y hat
prime, which is cosine alpha.

00:26:43.175 --> 00:26:48.775
Now the rule: new coordinate
number j is x sub i times C sub i j.

00:26:49.125 --> 00:26:53.401
The index i appears
twice, so, as in the vectors movie,

00:26:53.401 --> 00:26:57.600
we add up the terms for i equals
one and i equals two.

00:26:57.950 --> 00:27:02.335
For j equals one, that's x sub
one times C one one,

00:27:02.335 --> 00:27:05.000
plus x sub two times C two one.

00:27:05.350 --> 00:27:08.707
That's x cosine alpha plus y sine alpha:

00:27:08.707 --> 00:27:13.575
our x prime. With our numbers
that's four point six again.

00:27:15.075 --> 00:27:21.925
For j equals two, it's x sub one times C
one two, plus x sub two times C two two.

00:27:22.275 --> 00:27:26.097
That's minus x sine alpha
plus y cosine alpha:

00:27:26.097 --> 00:27:30.750
our y prime. With our numbers
that's one point nine six.

00:27:32.250 --> 00:27:36.575
So the short line says exactly
what our two long lines say.

00:27:36.925 --> 00:27:40.893
If we set the four C's
in a square, with i for the row and

00:27:40.893 --> 00:27:45.750
j for the column, we get our rotation
matrix flipped over its diagonal.

00:27:46.100 --> 00:27:51.064
That's only because this form reads down
the columns instead of along the rows,

00:27:51.064 --> 00:27:53.200
and the four numbers are the same.

00:27:53.550 --> 00:27:58.373
The same rule works in three dimensions,
and for things called tensors,

00:27:58.373 --> 00:28:02.925
which have their own movie later
in this series: Tensors made easy.

00:28:03.725 --> 00:28:06.700
Here's a nice link between the two ideas.

00:28:07.050 --> 00:28:10.357
Go back to the balloon,
at fifty three degrees,

00:28:10.357 --> 00:28:14.650
and turn the axes by alpha equals
theta, fifty three degrees.

00:28:15.000 --> 00:28:20.150
Then x hat prime is cosine
theta, sine theta, which is r hat.

00:28:20.500 --> 00:28:25.975
And y hat prime is minus sine
theta, cosine theta, which is theta hat.

00:28:27.475 --> 00:28:30.749
So r hat and theta hat
are just the east and

00:28:30.749 --> 00:28:33.725
north arrows, turned by the angle theta.

00:28:34.075 --> 00:28:39.350
That means the polar parts of any arrow
are its coordinates in turned axes.

00:28:39.700 --> 00:28:43.225
Let's check with the east wind, ten, zero.

00:28:43.575 --> 00:28:50.175
X prime is ten times zero point six, plus
zero times zero point eight, which is six.

00:28:51.675 --> 00:28:55.077
Y prime is minus ten
times zero point eight,

00:28:55.077 --> 00:28:59.175
plus zero times zero point six, which
is minus eight.

00:29:00.675 --> 00:29:05.175
Those are exactly u sub r and u sub
theta from before.

00:29:05.525 --> 00:29:09.200
Now the balloon's own
position arrow, three, four.

00:29:09.550 --> 00:29:14.975
X prime is three times zero point
six, plus four times zero point eight.

00:29:15.325 --> 00:29:19.300
That's one point eight
plus three point two, which is five.

00:29:20.800 --> 00:29:26.550
Y prime is minus three times zero point
eight, plus four times zero point six.

00:29:26.900 --> 00:29:31.375
That's minus two point four
plus two point four, which is zero.

00:29:32.875 --> 00:29:38.236
So the balloon is at five, zero in these
axes: five kilometres along r hat,

00:29:38.236 --> 00:29:43.525
and nothing along theta hat, because r hat
points straight at the balloon.

00:29:43.875 --> 00:29:46.050
There's one difference, though.

00:29:46.400 --> 00:29:50.675
With turned axes, we choose
one angle for the whole map.

00:29:51.025 --> 00:29:56.700
With polar coordinates, the angle is
theta, so it changes from place to place.

00:29:57.050 --> 00:29:59.513
If you skipped, here's what to keep:

00:29:59.513 --> 00:30:04.849
turning the axes by an angle alpha changes
the numbers that describe an arrow,

00:30:04.849 --> 00:30:09.775
but not the arrow itself, and polar
parts are just axes turned by theta.

00:30:10.575 --> 00:30:14.000
Now let's take polar
coordinates to a real storm.

00:30:14.350 --> 00:30:18.123
Here is Hurricane Ian
on the twenty eighth of September,

00:30:18.123 --> 00:30:20.885
twenty twenty-two, at six in the evening,

00:30:20.885 --> 00:30:26.275
universal time, an hour before it came
ashore on the southwest coast of Florida.

00:30:26.625 --> 00:30:30.640
The colours show the wind speed
ten metres above the ground,

00:30:30.640 --> 00:30:35.325
from a weather model analysis with grid
points every three kilometres.

00:30:35.675 --> 00:30:39.872
Like most weather data, it
gives each wind as two numbers:

00:30:39.872 --> 00:30:43.200
its eastward part u, and
its northward part v.

00:30:43.550 --> 00:30:46.725
The calm centre of the storm
is called the eye.

00:30:47.075 --> 00:30:50.950
Let's put the origin in the middle
of the eye, and zoom in.

00:30:51.300 --> 00:30:55.703
The eye sits just off
the coast, over the warm Gulf of Mexico,

00:30:55.703 --> 00:30:59.750
with the flat, low land
of southwest Florida to its east.

00:31:00.100 --> 00:31:03.975
Round the eye, the winds are
the strongest in the storm.

00:31:04.325 --> 00:31:08.289
Now every point round the eye
gets its polar coordinates:

00:31:08.289 --> 00:31:12.600
its distance r from the centre, and
its angle theta from east.

00:31:13.400 --> 00:31:16.900
Every wind on the map
now has two polar parts.

00:31:17.250 --> 00:31:21.025
The radial wind, u sub r, blows in or out.

00:31:21.375 --> 00:31:25.825
The tangential wind, u sub
theta, blows round the eye.

00:31:26.175 --> 00:31:30.010
Let's draw a ring twenty five
kilometres from the centre,

00:31:30.010 --> 00:31:35.325
and work out both parts at seventy two
points round it, one every five degrees.

00:31:35.675 --> 00:31:38.517
Then we average each part round the ring:

00:31:38.517 --> 00:31:42.675
we add up the seventy two
values, and divide by seventy two.

00:31:43.025 --> 00:31:48.700
The seventy two tangential winds add up
to about two thousand five hundred and

00:31:48.700 --> 00:31:52.047
six metres per second.
Divided by seventy two,

00:31:52.047 --> 00:31:57.650
that's about thirty four point eight, or
about thirty five metres per second.

00:31:59.150 --> 00:32:04.833
To turn metres per second into kilometres
per hour, we multiply by three point six,

00:32:04.833 --> 00:32:08.598
because an hour has three
thousand six hundred seconds,

00:32:08.598 --> 00:32:11.200
and a kilometre has a thousand metres.

00:32:11.550 --> 00:32:15.467
So thirty five metres per second
is about a hundred and

00:32:15.467 --> 00:32:17.675
twenty six kilometres per hour.

00:32:19.175 --> 00:32:23.206
The air goes round anticlockwise,
as seen from above,

00:32:23.206 --> 00:32:26.400
as in every northern hemisphere hurricane.

00:32:26.750 --> 00:32:31.721
The seventy two radial winds add
up to about minus nine hundred and

00:32:31.721 --> 00:32:34.392
fifty seven. Divided by seventy two,

00:32:34.392 --> 00:32:40.550
that's about minus thirteen point three,
or about minus thirteen metres per second.

00:32:42.050 --> 00:32:47.525
The minus sign means that, on average,
the air is flowing in, towards the eye.

00:32:47.875 --> 00:32:50.500
The real storm was even stronger.

00:32:50.850 --> 00:32:54.611
The official estimate
of its strongest wind at that hour,

00:32:54.611 --> 00:32:58.900
averaged over a minute, is about a hundred
and thirty five knots.

00:32:59.250 --> 00:33:03.152
One knot is about one point eight five
kilometres per hour,

00:33:03.152 --> 00:33:05.202
so that's about two hundred and

00:33:05.202 --> 00:33:09.500
fifty kilometres per hour, or about
sixty nine metres per second.

00:33:11.000 --> 00:33:13.315
That's the single strongest wind,

00:33:13.315 --> 00:33:16.892
while our thirty five
is an average round the ring,

00:33:16.892 --> 00:33:21.031
and even the strongest wind
on our model ring is only about

00:33:21.031 --> 00:33:25.450
forty nine. The model analysis
smooths out the strongest winds.

00:33:26.250 --> 00:33:30.100
So what do these two numbers
mean, and how are they different?

00:33:30.450 --> 00:33:34.075
Thirty five is how fast
the air goes round the eye.

00:33:34.425 --> 00:33:37.975
Thirteen is how fast it
moves towards the eye.

00:33:38.325 --> 00:33:42.900
They're two parts of the same
wind, at right angles to each other.

00:33:43.250 --> 00:33:49.063
By Pythagoras, together they make an arrow
of the square root of thirty five squared

00:33:49.063 --> 00:33:53.545
plus thirteen squared. Thirty
five squared is twelve hundred and

00:33:53.545 --> 00:33:56.976
twenty five, and thirteen
squared is a hundred and

00:33:56.976 --> 00:34:00.338
sixty nine. Together
that's thirteen hundred and

00:34:00.338 --> 00:34:05.450
ninety four, and its square root
is about thirty seven metres per second.

00:34:06.950 --> 00:34:10.175
It points mostly round, and a little in.

00:34:10.525 --> 00:34:14.050
Thirteen metres
per second, times three point six,

00:34:14.050 --> 00:34:18.350
is about forty seven kilometres
per hour, towards the centre.

00:34:19.850 --> 00:34:23.575
Now imagine,
just for a moment, that nothing changed.

00:34:23.925 --> 00:34:28.335
An air parcel, a small volume
of air that moves with the flow,

00:34:28.335 --> 00:34:31.750
starts on this ring, twenty
five kilometres out.

00:34:32.100 --> 00:34:34.993
Moving in at thirteen metres per second,

00:34:34.993 --> 00:34:38.320
it would have to cover
twenty five kilometres,

00:34:38.320 --> 00:34:42.516
which is twenty five thousand
metres. Twenty five thousand

00:34:42.516 --> 00:34:46.060
divided by thirteen is about
nineteen hundred and

00:34:46.060 --> 00:34:50.110
twenty three seconds.
Divided by sixty seconds a minute,

00:34:50.110 --> 00:34:52.425
that's about thirty two minutes.

00:34:53.925 --> 00:34:58.540
Going round at thirty five metres
per second, one lap of this ring,

00:34:58.540 --> 00:35:02.603
two pi times twenty five kilometres,
is about a hundred and

00:35:02.603 --> 00:35:07.493
fifty seven kilometres. That takes
about four thousand four hundred and

00:35:07.493 --> 00:35:11.075
ninety seconds, which
is about seventy five minutes.

00:35:12.575 --> 00:35:16.825
So the air is moving round
and moving in at the same time.

00:35:17.175 --> 00:35:22.873
In any stretch of time, it goes thirty
five metres round for every thirteen in,

00:35:22.873 --> 00:35:25.902
and thirty five over thirteen is about two

00:35:25.902 --> 00:35:29.076
point seven. So for every
metre it moves in,

00:35:29.076 --> 00:35:31.600
it moves almost three metres round.

00:35:33.100 --> 00:35:37.100
The air doesn't fall straight
into the eye: it spirals in.

00:35:37.450 --> 00:35:42.897
In the real storm, the inflow slows
down near the ring of strongest winds,

00:35:42.897 --> 00:35:46.725
and the air rises there instead,
in towering clouds.

00:35:47.075 --> 00:35:49.820
We'll look at that rising air in Part 2,

00:35:49.820 --> 00:35:55.447
and the whole story of how hurricanes work
has its own movie later in this series:

00:35:55.447 --> 00:35:57.300
How tropical cyclones form.

00:35:58.100 --> 00:36:01.245
Next, we work out three
ring points by hand,

00:36:01.245 --> 00:36:04.175
for anyone who wants to see every number.

00:36:04.525 --> 00:36:08.175
You don't need these steps
for the intuition of weather,

00:36:08.175 --> 00:36:09.739
climate science or maps,

00:36:09.739 --> 00:36:13.975
so if that feels like too much, skip
ahead to the time on screen.

00:36:14.325 --> 00:36:16.875
Where do those averages come from?

00:36:17.225 --> 00:36:20.850
Let's work out three
of the seventy two points by hand.

00:36:21.200 --> 00:36:26.500
Remember, the data give us u and v, the
eastward and northward parts.

00:36:26.850 --> 00:36:31.150
First, the point due east
of the eye, at theta equals zero.

00:36:31.500 --> 00:36:35.612
There, u is minus
eight point seven, and v is thirty eight:

00:36:35.612 --> 00:36:38.400
the wind blows north, and a little west.

00:36:39.900 --> 00:36:45.350
Our rule has cosine of zero, which
is one, and sine of zero, which is zero.

00:36:45.700 --> 00:36:51.200
So u sub r equals u, which
is minus eight point seven: a little in.

00:36:51.550 --> 00:36:55.975
And u sub theta equals v, which
is thirty eight: round.

00:36:56.325 --> 00:36:59.275
Here, the wind blows mostly round.

00:37:00.775 --> 00:37:04.900
Next, the point due north
of the eye, at ninety degrees.

00:37:05.250 --> 00:37:10.827
There, u is minus sixteen point nine, and
v is minus thirty seven point four:

00:37:10.827 --> 00:37:15.100
the wind blows mostly towards
the south, and a little west.

00:37:16.600 --> 00:37:20.200
Now the cosine is zero
and the sine is one.

00:37:20.550 --> 00:37:26.275
So u sub r equals v, which
is minus thirty seven point four: in.

00:37:26.625 --> 00:37:29.751
And u sub theta is minus u times one,

00:37:29.751 --> 00:37:33.469
plus v times zero.
Minus times minus sixteen

00:37:33.469 --> 00:37:37.525
point nine makes
plus sixteen point nine: round.

00:37:39.025 --> 00:37:44.200
Last, the point to the northwest,
at a hundred and thirty five degrees.

00:37:44.550 --> 00:37:50.050
There, u is minus ten point one, and v is
minus forty four point five.

00:37:51.550 --> 00:37:56.676
A hundred and thirty five degrees
is forty five degrees short of due west,

00:37:56.676 --> 00:38:00.485
so its cosine is minus one
over the square root of two,

00:38:00.485 --> 00:38:03.256
about minus zero point seven zero seven,

00:38:03.256 --> 00:38:06.650
and its sine is plus
zero point seven zero seven.

00:38:07.000 --> 00:38:12.996
So u sub r is minus ten point one times
minus zero point seven zero seven,

00:38:12.996 --> 00:38:18.425
plus minus forty four point five
times zero point seven zero seven.

00:38:18.775 --> 00:38:23.125
That's seven point one four
minus thirty one point four six,

00:38:23.125 --> 00:38:26.025
about minus twenty four point three: in.

00:38:27.525 --> 00:38:30.426
And u sub theta is minus u times sine,

00:38:30.426 --> 00:38:34.855
so minus minus ten point one, which
is plus ten point one,

00:38:34.855 --> 00:38:37.451
times zero point seven zero seven,

00:38:37.451 --> 00:38:43.025
plus minus forty four point five times
minus zero point seven zero seven.

00:38:43.375 --> 00:38:47.722
That's seven point one four
plus thirty one point four six,

00:38:47.722 --> 00:38:50.375
about thirty eight point six: round.

00:38:51.875 --> 00:38:55.831
The computer does exactly this
at all seventy two points,

00:38:55.831 --> 00:38:57.775
and then takes the averages.

00:38:58.125 --> 00:39:00.407
If you skipped, here's what to keep:

00:39:00.407 --> 00:39:03.577
at every point round
the ring, the wind's east and

00:39:03.577 --> 00:39:06.303
north parts turn into
an in or out part and

00:39:06.303 --> 00:39:08.775
a round part, and then we average them.

00:39:09.575 --> 00:39:12.400
Why bother with polar coordinates here?

00:39:12.750 --> 00:39:17.925
Let's go all the way round the ring, and
plot the winds against the angle theta.

00:39:18.275 --> 00:39:22.063
First, the eastward
wind u, the solid orange line,

00:39:22.063 --> 00:39:25.775
and the northward
wind v, the dashed orange line.

00:39:26.125 --> 00:39:28.675
Our three points sit on these lines.

00:39:29.025 --> 00:39:34.900
Both lines swing up and down, from plus
to minus, by tens of metres per second.

00:39:35.250 --> 00:39:39.875
They swing because the wind keeps
turning as we go round the eye.

00:39:40.225 --> 00:39:44.612
On the east side it blows
north, on the west side it blows south,

00:39:44.612 --> 00:39:48.325
and on the south side it
blows east, all the way round.

00:39:48.675 --> 00:39:52.350
Now let's plot the two polar
parts round the same ring.

00:39:52.700 --> 00:39:56.655
The tangential wind, u sub
theta, is the teal line,

00:39:56.655 --> 00:40:00.300
and the radial
wind, u sub r, is the rose line.

00:40:00.650 --> 00:40:04.434
The tangential wind stays
positive all the way round,

00:40:04.434 --> 00:40:09.575
between about seventeen and forty
seven, with an average of thirty five.

00:40:09.925 --> 00:40:14.143
The radial wind wobbles more,
between about minus forty and

00:40:14.143 --> 00:40:18.075
plus fourteen, because
the storm isn't perfectly round.

00:40:18.425 --> 00:40:21.941
It's negative at fifty eight
of the seventy two points,

00:40:21.941 --> 00:40:25.265
and only on the southwest
side does it blow outward,

00:40:25.265 --> 00:40:27.950
by up to about fourteen metres per second.

00:40:28.300 --> 00:40:31.300
So, on average, the air flows in.

00:40:31.650 --> 00:40:35.265
Instead of two lines that
swing from plus to minus,

00:40:35.265 --> 00:40:38.030
we get two numbers that mean something:

00:40:38.030 --> 00:40:42.000
how fast the storm spins, and
how fast the air flows in.

00:40:42.350 --> 00:40:46.350
That's why hurricane scientists
use polar coordinates.

00:40:47.150 --> 00:40:50.800
There's one more angle that
hurricane scientists use.

00:40:51.150 --> 00:40:55.150
The wind crosses each ring
at a slant, a little inward.

00:40:55.500 --> 00:40:59.402
The angle between the wind
and the ring's own direction,

00:40:59.402 --> 00:41:02.050
theta hat, is called the inflow angle.

00:41:02.400 --> 00:41:06.798
Its tangent is the side opposite
the angle over the side next to it:

00:41:06.798 --> 00:41:09.126
the inward part over the round part,

00:41:09.126 --> 00:41:14.300
thirteen point three over thirty four
point eight, about zero point three eight.

00:41:14.650 --> 00:41:20.350
So the inflow angle, the angle with that
tangent, is about twenty one degrees.

00:41:21.850 --> 00:41:26.475
We've now met four different
angles, so let's put them side by side.

00:41:26.825 --> 00:41:32.800
Theta says where a point is, measured from
east, anticlockwise as seen from above.

00:41:33.150 --> 00:41:36.617
The compass bearing says
which way something points,

00:41:36.617 --> 00:41:39.950
measured from north,
clockwise as seen from above.

00:41:40.300 --> 00:41:43.400
Alpha says how far we turned our axes.

00:41:43.750 --> 00:41:48.725
And the inflow angle says how far
the wind leans inward from the ring.

00:41:49.075 --> 00:41:51.278
They answer four different questions,

00:41:51.278 --> 00:41:54.850
so it's always worth checking
which angle you're looking at.

00:41:55.200 --> 00:41:59.425
The storm's spin also has its own
measure, called vorticity,

00:41:59.425 --> 00:42:04.425
which has its own movie later in this
series: Vorticity in plain words.

00:42:05.225 --> 00:42:09.147
To sum up, coordinates are
numbers that pin down a place,

00:42:09.147 --> 00:42:12.450
measured from an origin
along chosen directions.

00:42:12.800 --> 00:42:17.223
Cartesian coordinates
use x and y, and their unit vectors,

00:42:17.223 --> 00:42:20.350
x hat and y hat, are the same everywhere.

00:42:20.700 --> 00:42:25.252
Polar coordinates use
the distance r and the angle theta,

00:42:25.252 --> 00:42:29.725
with x equals r cosine
theta, and y equals r sine theta.

00:42:30.075 --> 00:42:34.375
The arc length is the radius
times the angle, in radians.

00:42:34.725 --> 00:42:37.375
R hat points the way r grows.

00:42:37.725 --> 00:42:40.553
Theta hat points the way theta grows:

00:42:40.553 --> 00:42:45.750
it's r hat turned by ninety degrees,
minus sine theta, cosine theta.

00:42:46.100 --> 00:42:50.284
The parts of a wind along these
two arrows are dot products:

00:42:50.284 --> 00:42:52.725
the radial and the tangential wind.

00:42:53.075 --> 00:42:58.343
Turning the axes by an angle alpha gives
new coordinates for the same arrow,

00:42:58.343 --> 00:43:02.225
and the four direction cosines
make the rotation matrix.

00:43:02.575 --> 00:43:08.275
Polar parts are just turned axes, with an
angle that changes from place to place.

00:43:08.625 --> 00:43:10.420
Round Hurricane Ian's eye,

00:43:10.420 --> 00:43:15.392
they split the wind into about thirty
five metres per second of spin and

00:43:15.392 --> 00:43:20.225
thirteen of inflow, so the air spirals
in at about twenty one degrees.

00:43:20.575 --> 00:43:25.986
In Part 2, we'll ask whether air going
round at a steady speed is accelerating,

00:43:25.986 --> 00:43:29.000
and look at a hurricane
in three dimensions.

00:43:29.800 --> 00:43:33.958
This video is one of a series
of related maths tutorials,

00:43:33.958 --> 00:43:36.000
so watch out for the others.

00:43:36.350 --> 00:43:40.747
They're made for students
of atmospheric science, meteorology and

00:43:40.747 --> 00:43:44.400
fluid dynamics, and anyone
else who needs these ideas.

00:43:44.750 --> 00:43:49.578
The notebook for coordinates, and the
notebooks for the other maths concepts,

00:43:49.578 --> 00:43:52.400
are on the website that
goes with this video.
